= Nehru's Foreign Policy: The Architecture of Independence

Free India inherited no army of diplomats and no tradition of foreign policy — Nehru built both from scratch, serving as his own foreign minister. His architecture rested on one refusal and three commitments, and every later event in this chapter tests how far that architecture could bend without breaking.

- *The refusal — no blocs in a bipolar world:*
  - By 1950 the world had split into two armed camps — the US-led Western bloc and the Soviet-led Eastern bloc — each demanding loyalty, bases and wars.
  - India's answer: *non-alignment* — join NEITHER camp, judge every issue on its merits, and keep the right to say yes or no independently.
  - *What it is NOT (the classic trap):* non-alignment is not neutrality. A neutral state stays passive on every issue; a non-aligned state takes POSITIONS — India backed decolonisation, opposed Suez 1956, and sided with Bangladesh's people in 1971.

- *The commitments:*
  - *Anti-colonial solidarity:* India championed independence movements across Asia and Africa — the 1955 *Bandung* Afro-Asian conference made Nehru the senior voice of the newly free world.
  - *Peaceful settlement:* disputes would go to negotiation and the UN, not to war — the principle India's own three wars would test.
  - *The Commonwealth solution:* India stayed in the Commonwealth as a REPUBLIC (1949 London declaration) — membership without dominion status, another Nehruvian first.
  - *Institutional voice:* UN peacekeeping participation and mediation (Korea) — a small power acting like a responsible world citizen.

- *Panchsheel — the five principles (29 April 1954):*
  - Signed with CHINA (the Tibet-region trade agreement's preamble) — five rules of co-existence: mutual respect for sovereignty and territorial integrity; mutual non-aggression; non-interference in internal affairs; equality and mutual benefit; peaceful co-existence.
  - *The irony every exam mines:* the treaty partner was the country that attacked India within eight years, making a mockery of principles one and two.
  - *Why it still matters:* Panchsheel became the NAM's moral vocabulary — the code of conduct the non-aligned world quoted for decades.

- *The NAM institutionalisation:*
  - Founding leaders: *Nehru (India), Nasser (Egypt), Tito (Yugoslavia), Sukarno (Indonesia), Nkrumah (Ghana)*.
  - *Belgrade, 1961:* the first Non-Aligned Movement summit — 25 countries; the movement became a standing third voice in world politics.
  - *The bargain it bought India:* arms, aid and technology from BOTH blocs — Western food and Eastern steel — while keeping the flag and the judgment independent.

#analogy-box(title: "The Household That Refuses Both Landlords")[
  Two competing landlords own every shop on the street and want every tenant on their side — discounts for loyalty, evictions for defiance. One tenant buys from whoever offers the better price, signs no exclusivity deal with anyone, and still speaks up when either landlord bullies a neighbour. The mapping: landlords = the Cold War blocs; buying on merit = arms/aid from both sides (Soviet steel, American wheat); speaking up = India's anti-colonial campaigns. The tenant is NOT neutral about injustice — he is simply nobody's client. That is non-alignment.
]

= The Sino-Indian War of 1962: The Fall of the Brotherhood

"Hindi-Chini bhai-bhai" — Indians and Chinese are brothers — was the slogan of the 1950s. October 1962 turned it into a national wound, and the syllabus lists this war first for a reason: it changed India's defence policy, its politics and its view of the world.

- *The ladder to war (learn each rung):*
  - *1950 — Tibet absorbed:* China takes over Tibet, bordering India directly; Delhi protests weakly and accepts the fact.
  - *1954 — Panchsheel:* the friendship treaty (see above) — good feeling at its peak.
  - *Mid-1950s — the map quarrel:* China publishes maps claiming Indian areas; India objects. The core dispute has two fronts: *Aksai Chin* in eastern Ladakh (China quietly built a road through it linking Tibet to Xinjiang) and the *McMahon Line* in the North-East Frontier Agency (NEFA), which China never accepted.
  - *1959 — the rupture:* Tibet revolts; the *Dalai Lama flees to India and is given asylum*; Beijing reads Delhi as hostile. Border clashes begin.
  - *1960-62 — talks fail:* Nehru's "Forward Policy" posts small Indian units ahead of Chinese positions; neither side steps back.

- *The war (October-November 1962):*
  - *20 October 1962:* China attacks SIMULTANEOUSLY on both fronts — NEFA and Ladakh. The blitz shatters unprepared Indian defences; supplies never reached frozen forward posts.
  - *20 November 1962:* China declares a unilateral ceasefire and withdraws from NEFA — but *keeps Aksai Chin*, which remains Chinese-held to this day.
  - *The political casualty:* Defence Minister *V.K. Krishna Menon resigns* (Nehru's closest aide) — the first head claimed by the war.
  - *The national lessons:* the army was rebuilt and expanded; defence spending jumped; and the humiliation became the standard against which every later military event was measured.

- *The consequences beyond the battlefield:*
  - *Alliance logic shifted:* the US and UK airlifted emergency arms — India's first brush with Western military dependence.
  - *Domestic politics hardened:* criticism of Nehru's China policy grew; the army chief's authority rose; the myth of Panchsheel lay in ruins.
  - *The phrase to quote:* 1962 ended India's innocence about China — suspicion became permanent policy equipment.

= The Indo-Pak War of 1965: Shastri's Trial

Nehru died in May 1964; *Lal Bahadur Shastri* inherited the Pakistan quarrel — and within eighteen months, a full war. Learn this one as a three-act chain.

- *Act one — the spark:*
  - *Rann of Kutch, spring 1965:* border clashes in the salt marshes; a British-brokered ceasefire cools it — but Pakistan reads India as still weak after 1962.
  - *August 1965 — Operation Gibraltar:* Pakistan infiltrates guerrillas into Kashmir to spark an uprising; Kashmiris do not rise; India strikes the infiltration routes.

- *Act two — the war (September 1965):*
  - Pakistan launches a armoured thrust at *Khem Karan* in Punjab (Patton tanks) while India drives toward *Lahore*; the biggest tank battles since World War II follow at *Asal Uttar* — India's defences bog the Pattons down in flooded sugarcane country.
  - *The verdict on the ground:* India holds Kashmir and threatens Lahore; both sides claim victory, but Pakistan's strategic aim — seizing Kashmir — fails.

- *Act three — the peace and the price:*
  - *Tashkent Agreement (10 January 1966):* Soviet-brokered; India and Pakistan agree to return to pre-war positions — the status quo ante.
  - *11 January 1966:* *Shastri dies in Tashkent* of a heart attack, hours after signing — a national shock that becomes its own exam question.
  - *The consequence:* Indira Gandhi replaces him as Prime Minister — the next chapter's story begins here.

= The Indo-Pak War of 1971: The Birth of Bangladesh

The third war was the only one India fought to a decisive, world-changing finish — and it began inside Pakistan's own politics. Learn the chain: crackdown → refugees → treaty → war → victory → Shimla.

- *The trigger — Pakistan's self-wound:*
  - *December 1970 elections:* Sheikh Mujibur Rahman's *Awami League* wins an absolute majority in Pakistan's parliament — but East Pakistanis are Bengalis, and the West Pakistani military refuses to hand them power.
  - *25 March 1971 — Operation Searchlight:* the army cracks down on Dhaka; mass arrests and massacres follow; Mujib is jailed. The political crisis becomes a liberation war (the *Mukti Bahini* resistance).

- *The refugee flood:*
  - *Nearly 80 lakh refugees* stream into India — West Bengal, Assam, Tripura buckle under camps; the burden becomes a humanitarian and economic emergency.
  - *India's dilemma:* absorb quietly (impossible at this scale) or act. Indira Gandhi tours world capitals for months seeking pressure on Pakistan — and buys time while the army prepares.

- *The alliance shift — the treaty (August 1971):*
  - The *Indo-Soviet Treaty of Peace, Friendship and Cooperation* — a *20-year* pact guaranteeing mutual consultation and support.
  - *Why it mattered:* with the USSR standing behind India, China and the US could not intervene freely; a US task force did enter the Bay of Bengal in December — and stayed symbolic.
  - *The exam phrase:* this treaty is the syllabus's "shifting alliance" — from solo non-alignment to a formal partnership born of necessity.

- *The war and the victory (3-16 December 1971):*
  - Pakistan strikes airfields in the west first (3 December); India responds on BOTH fronts — while the eastern command, with the Mukti Bahini, races to Dhaka.
  - *16 December 1971:* General Niazi surrenders *93,000* prisoners in Dhaka — the largest surrender since World War II; *Bangladesh is born*.
  - *The verdict:* India's first clear-cut victory; non-alignment bent into a Soviet partnership without breaking; Indira Gandhi's stature peaks.

- *The settlement — Shimla (July 1972):*
  - *Indira Gandhi and Z.A. Bhutto* meet at Shimla; both agree to settle disputes peacefully and respect the ceasefire line — now renamed the *Line of Control (LoC)* in Kashmir.
  - *The lasting terms:* the LoC vocabulary and bilateral-negotiation principle flow from this agreement — quote both in treaty questions.

= India's Nuclear Programme: From Peaceful Atom to Pokhran

- *The foundation (1940s-60s):*
  - *Atomic Energy Commission, 1948* — Nehru and *Homi Bhabha* build the nuclear estate early: research reactors, the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (the old Tata Institute renamed 1967), nuclear power stations planned for a power-starved economy.
  - *The doctrine:* atoms for peace and development — India's nuclear identity BEGAN civilian.

- *The shock context:*
  - *1962's defeat, 1965's war, China's 1964 nuclear test* — the strategic neighbourhood turned nuclear and hostile within a decade; the bomb debate entered Indian politics.
  - *The NPT (1968):* a treaty dividing the world into nuclear "haves" (who keep their weapons) and "have-nots" (who renounce them forever). *India refused to sign* — the inequality was permanent and unacceptable; the same logic later rejected the CTBT.

- *Pokhran-I — 18 May 1974:*
  - *The event:* India detonates its first device at *Pokhran, Rajasthan* — codename *Smiling Buddha*.
  - *The official line:* a *PEACEFUL NUCLEAR EXPLOSION* — a capability demonstration, not a weapons announcement. The distinction is the exact phrase examiners hunt.
  - *The aftermath:* technology sanctions from the West; India settles into its long "nuclear ambiguity" — no arsenal declared, option preserved, universal disarmament still demanded.
  - *Looking forward:* Pokhran-II (May 1998) ends the ambiguity — but that belongs to the later chapters of the book.

= Shifting Alliances: How India Moved in a Moving World

- *The 1950s — courted by both camps:*
  - Soviet steel plants (Bhilai), American wheat (PL-480), Western and Eastern technology — non-alignment maximised India's take from both sides.
  - *Pakistan's counter-move:* joining US pacts — *SEATO (1954)* and *CENTO* — American arms and bases, which pushed India defensively closer to Moscow for equipment.

- *The 1960s — the tilt becomes visible:*
  - *1962:* the US and UK airlift small arms during the China war — a flash of Western partnership.
  - *1965:* the US arms embargo hits India (dependent on Western gear) harder than Soviet-equipped Pakistan; the *USSR brokers Tashkent* — Moscow is now the reliable partner.
  - *Soviet arms and the veto:* Moscow's UN vetoes shield India on Kashmir through the decade.

- *1971 — the formal shift:*
  - The *Indo-Soviet Treaty* (above) hardens friendship into commitment for twenty years — the defining alliance fact of the era and the answer to "shifting alliance" questions.
  - *The evaluation line to quote:* the shift was *responsive, not ideological* — each Indian move answered a rival's move (US-Pakistan pacts → India-Soviet arms; 1971's US-China tilt → the treaty). Non-alignment adapted to a changed world rather than dying.

