= Detailed Solutions

Each solution shows the reasoning, not just the verdict — cover the answer line, attempt the question, then compare.

#solution(1, [Panchsheel — 29 April 1954 — formed the preamble of the India-China Tibet trade agreement. The Soviet pacts came in 1971; Nepal is a neighbouring treaty partner of a different kind.], answer: [B])

#solution(2, [Belgrade, Yugoslavia, September 1961 — the first NAM summit of 25 countries. Bandung (1955) was the Afro-Asian gathering that PREPARED the ground — a favourite near-miss option.], answer: [C])

#solution(3, [V.K. Krishna Menon — Nehru's closest defence aide — resigned on 6 November 1962 as the military collapse peaked. Y.B. Chavan succeeded him.], answer: [B])

#solution(4, [Lal Bahadur Shastri led India through 1965 ("Jai Jawan Jai Kisan"). Nehru died in 1964; Indira Gandhi took over only in January 1966 after Shastri's death at Tashkent.], answer: [D])

#solution(5, [The Indo-Soviet Treaty of Peace, Friendship and Cooperation — August 1971, twenty years — signed BEFORE the December war to deter US-China intervention.], answer: [B])

#solution(6, [16 December 1971 — the day General Niazi signed the surrender in Dhaka. March 1971 marks the crackdown that STARTED the crisis; July 1972 is Shimla.], answer: [C])

#solution(7, [18 May 1974 — Pokhran-I, codename "Smiling Buddha", officially a "peaceful nuclear explosion". 1968 is the NPT year and 1998 is Pokhran-II — the deliberate bookend distractors.], answer: [C])

#solution(8, [India's stated objection: the NPT froze a permanent divide — the weapons states keep theirs, everyone else renounces forever. The refusal logic also covered the CTBT later.], answer: [A])

#solution(9, [The Line of Control — the 1971 ceasefire line renamed at Shimla. The Durand, Radcliffe and McMahon Lines are colonial-era boundaries — classic distractors.], answer: [D])

#solution(10, [Niazi surrendered to Lt Gen Jagjit Singh Aurora, GOC-in-C Eastern Command — the alliance with the Mukti Bahini was Aurora's building. Manekshaw was the Army Chief who planned and timed the campaign.], answer: [A])

